# Normalise line endings so source-pinning tests behave identically on every
# platform.
#
# Why this file exists: several seam guards read REAL source files from disk and
# assert on them with regexes. Some of those regexes are $-anchored, and in
# JavaScript `$` matches end-of-STRING, not end-of-LINE. On a CRLF checkout each
# line therefore ends with "\r", so a pattern like /^const FOO =$/ can never match
# even though the source is byte-for-byte correct.
#
# That produced a real, deterministic, platform-dependent failure:
#   apps/dashboard/server/__tests__/ws3CeremonySeamGuard.test.mjs:255
#     expect(src.split("\n").find(l => /^const RAIL_OFF_TESTNET_ONLY =$/.test(l)))
#   The line on disk was "const RAIL_OFF_TESTNET_ONLY =\r", so `find` returned
#   undefined and the assertion failed with:
#     expected undefined to be 'const RAIL_OFF_TESTNET_ONLY ='
#
# The file had been checked out with CRLF because core.autocrlf was true and
# nothing in the repo normalised anything. The blob in git was always correct LF;
# only the working tree differed. That is why this is invisible in CI and only
# reproduces on a Windows checkout with autocrlf enabled.
#
# Two rules, deliberately conservative:
#   - text=auto           normalise text files, leave binaries alone
#   - *.mjs  text eol=lf  JavaScript is the class these guards read; force LF so
#                         a Windows checkout cannot reintroduce CRLF
#
# Shell and Markdown are left to text=auto rather than being pinned, so this file
# does not create a large whitespace-only diff in unrelated files.
* text=auto

*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
